0,Lokanga bara - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lokanga_bara,The lokanga bara (sometimes referred to by the generic term lokanga) is a three-stringed fiddle popular among the Southern Antandroy and Bara ethnic groups of Madagascar.,"Malagasy, Bara",Austronesian
1,Music of Madagascar - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Madagascar,"For instance, in the Highlands, the valiha and more subdued vocal styles are emblematic of the Merina, the predominantly Austronesian ethnic group that has inhabited the area since at least the 15th century, whereas among the southern Bara people, who trace their ancestry back to the African mainland, their a cappella vocal traditions bear ...","Malagasy, Bara",Austronesian
2,Lokanga bara — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/lokanga_bara,"Etymology and Naming The term lokanga in Malagasy denotes a bowed string instrument, akin to a fiddle or violin. [4] This general nomenclature applies to various regional variants across Madagascar, reflecting the instrument's role in traditional music. The specific designation lokanga bara identifies the version prevalent among the Bara ethnic group in southern Madagascar, where ""bara ...","Malagasy, Bara",Austronesian
3,Qui sont les Bara ? - Ethno Mada,https://ethnomada.wordpress.com/2025/05/13/les-bara/,"Qui sont les Bara ? Peuple du sud malagasy, les Bara sont connus pour leurs traditions pastorales, leurs chants polyphoniques et leurs rites de passage marqués. Leur identité complexe mêle pratiques ancestrales et influences historiques variées. Et si nous découvrions ensemble ce peuple encore peu documenté dans les ouvrages généralistes ?","Malagasy, Bara",Austronesian
4,Traditional music in Madagascar,https://www.musicinafrica.net/magazine/traditional-music-madagascar/,"Traditional music in Madagascar formed its identity with influences from various horizons: the Austronesian peoples on the one hand, and African, Arab and European migratory populations on the other. Such variety made it impossible to specify, in time and space, the true origin of traditional music in Madagascar. What is certain is that traditional music was probably heard for the first time ...","Malagasy, Bara",Austronesian
5,Music of Madagascar — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Music_of_Madagascar,"The music of Madagascar comprises the diverse traditional and contemporary styles performed by the Malagasy people, reflecting the island's settlement by Austronesian migrants from Southeast Asia around the 5th to 10th centuries CE and subsequent Bantu arrivals from East Africa, which produced a distinctive synthesis of melodic chordophones and rhythmic percussion. [1][2] Traditional Malagasy ...","Malagasy, Bara",Austronesian
